WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.930
Shine a laser through two narrow
slits, very close together,

2
00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:05.760
onto a distant wall.

3
00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:08.400
A row of bright and dark stripes appears,

4
00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:10.090
with a bright one in the middle.

5
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:12.410
Here is the light's wavelength.

6
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:18.130
Count the zeros after the decimal
point: six of them, then 633.

7
00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.060
Far too small to see.

8
00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:23.550
Yet the stripes are big enough to
measure with an ordinary ruler.

9
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:27.860
So how can stripes on a wall
measure a wavelength that small?

10
00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:31.720
In this mission, you'll see how
light spreads out from each slit,

11
00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.940
overlaps, and interferes.

12
00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:38.260
Then you'll work through a real
example, from laser to stripes.

13
00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:43.390
Your mission: find out how stripes on a
wall can measure a wavelength that small.
